# Student Performance Analysis: Statistical Testing and Regression in R

An end-to-end statistical analysis project exploring factors associated with students' final mathematics grades using R.

## Project Overview

This project analyzes student performance data to explore relationships between student characteristics, study habits, school-related factors, and final mathematics grades.

The analysis combines exploratory data analysis (EDA), hypothesis testing, correlation analysis, multiple linear regression, and model diagnostics to produce clear, evidence-based statistical insights.

## Objectives

- Understand the structure and quality of the dataset.
- Explore distributions and patterns in student mathematics grades.
- Compare grades across selected student groups.
- Examine the relationship between absences and final grades.
- Build and evaluate a multiple linear regression model.
- Communicate findings through visualizations and a Quarto report.

## Dataset

- **Source:** [UCI Student Performance Dataset](https://archive.ics.uci.edu/dataset/320/student+performance)
- **File:** `student-mat.csv`
- **Observations:** 395 students
- **Variables:** 33
- **Target variable:** `G3` — final mathematics grade

The original dataset uses a semicolon (`;`) as its field delimiter.

## Tools & Techniques

**Tools**
- R
- Tidyverse
- Quarto
- RStudio

**Analytical techniques**
- Data understanding and quality assessment
- Data cleaning and exploratory data analysis
- Welch's two-sample t-test
- Kruskal–Wallis test
- Spearman rank correlation
- Multiple linear regression
- Regression diagnostics

## Key Findings

- The baseline regression model explains approximately **17.1% of the variation** in final mathematics grades (R² = 0.171).
- Previous academic failures are negatively associated with final grades in the fitted model.
- The male category has a positive estimated coefficient relative to the female reference category, holding the other included predictors constant.
- Students reporting 5–10 hours of weekly study time have a positive estimated coefficient relative to the reference study-time category.
- The model has limited explanatory power, indicating that other factors not included in the baseline model may also be important.

These findings describe statistical associations, not causal effects. Results should be interpreted in the context of the dataset and model assumptions.

